 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** The renderer consistently provides above 150 FPS for tested datasets (bunny_cloud, explosion, fire, utahteapot, venusstatue, dragon) across all LODs, even with millions of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Footprint:**
-    *   **Dragon Dataset (High LOD):** 546 MB, 23.3M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-    *   **Dragon Dataset (Low LOD):** 174.4 MB, 7.4M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Memory usage depends on the ratio of spheres (1 float covariance) to ellipsoids (3 floats covariance) and whether opacity is stored per Gaussian or uniform (level sets) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance via hardware-accelerated ray tracing (OptiX); memory efficiency through sparse VDB representation and LOD; support for transfer functions [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Lossy representation due to non-overlapping constraint; limited to three LOD levels in current implementation; NanoVDB cannot be used; recursive tracing limited by register constraints [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Scientific visualization (SciVis) of large sparse volumetric datasets where real-time performance is critical and photorealistic overlapping Gaussian blending is not required [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** Photorealistic rendering requiring overlapping Gaussian mixture models; scenes requiring NanoVDB integration; applications needing fine-grained, non-LOD-based opacity-independent truncation [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering is accelerated by OptiX hardware ray tracing. Performance is consistently above 150 FPS for tested datasets (bunny_cloud, explosion, fire, utahteapot, venusstatue, dragon) across all LODs, even with millions of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory footprint depends on LOD and dataset sparsity.
+    *   **Dragon Dataset (High LOD):** 546MB, 23.3M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+    *   **Dragon Dataset (Low LOD):** 174.4MB, 7.4M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+    *   Storage includes global position (3 floats), covariance (1 float for spheres, 3 for ellipsoids), and opacity (1 float) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance via hardware acceleration; compatibility with OpenVDB's sparse storage; support for transfer functions; unified representation of volumes and level-sets [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Lossy representation due to non-overlapping constraint; limited to three LOD levels; NanoVDB incompatibility requires CPU-side generation; OptiX register constraints limit recursive state propagation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Scientific visualization (SciVis) where real-time performance and LOD support are prioritized over photorealistic accuracy.
+*   **When not to use:** Photorealistic rendering requiring overlapping Gaussians or shadow/scattering rays; scenarios where GPU-side VDB traversal is required (NanoVDB cannot be used) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in a study for Scientific Visualization (SciVis) that explores the integration of OpenVDB and NVIDIA OptiX [S1, unofficial]. It builds on the popularity of 3D Gaussian approximation in stereoscopic image-to-3D scene conversion (Gaussian Splatting) [S1, unofficial]. The work distinguishes itself from previous Gaussian mixture models in SciVis (e.g., Jang et al.) by leveraging OpenVDB's memory compaction and OptiX's hardware acceleration [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to handle large challenging datasets efficiently, moving away from dense scalar field rendering [S1, unofficial].
+
+*   **Origin:** The method was introduced in the paper "3D Gaussian Particle Approximation of VDB Datasets: A Study for Scientific Visualization" [S1, unofficial].
+*   **History:** It builds on the popularity of 3D Gaussian approximation in stereoscopic image-to-3D scene conversion (Gaussian Splatting) [12] and earlier SciVis work by Jang et al. [11]. The work integrates OpenVDB and NVIDIA OptiX to leverage memory compaction and hardware acceleration [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Used in scientific visualization pipelines for rendering large sparse volumetric datasets in real-time [S1, unofficial].
 
